`Deactivate()` calls:
```cppvoid Deactivate()
{
 if (rosidl_pub_) {
 rosidl_pub_->on_deactivate();
 }
 if (pub_) {
 pub_->on_deactivate();
 }
}
```

Hmm, that should work. But wait — the timestamp is1794047749 which is ~6 seconds after1794047743. So the message came after deactivate.

Wait. Maybe `on_deactivate` on LifecyclePublisher... Let me check. Actually let me look at what `on_deactivate` does in LifecyclePublisher:

Looking at the header earlier:
```cpp void on_activate() override {
 SimpleManagedEntity::on_activate();
 should_log_ = true;
 }
```

And on_deactivate is probably in SimpleManagedEntity. But the key question: does `publish()` check `is_activated()`? Yes:
```cpp virtual void publish(const MessageT & msg)
 {
 if (!this->is_activated()) {
 log_publisher_not_enabled();
 return;
 }
```
So if on_deactivate was called, publish should be dropped.

